\title{Anti-Mathematics: Negating the Axioms of Set Theory,\\
with a Complete Development of the Finite Universe}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We investigate \emph{anti-mathematics}: the systematic study of the theories
obtained by negating individual axioms of Zermelo--Fraenkel set theory (ZF).
Our principal contribution is a complete, self-contained development of the
theory $\mathrm{ZF} - \mathrm{Infinity} + \neg\mathrm{Infinity}$, the theory of
\emph{hereditarily finite sets} $\HF = V_\omega$, realized concretely in the
\emph{Ackermann model} whose carrier is $\mathbb{N}$ and whose membership
relation is $a \meml b :\Longleftrightarrow \bit_a(b) = 1$, reading a number as
the finite set of positions of the $1$s in its binary expansion. We prove that
in this model every ZF axiom other than Infinity holds---the finite axioms
(Empty, Pairing, Union, Power Set, Foundation), the $\in$-Induction schema, the
Separation schema, and the Replacement schema---with all schema witnesses
constructed explicitly as binary bitmasks. We then prove that negating Infinity
\emph{settles} the status of the Axiom of Choice: whereas Choice is independent
of full ZF, in the finite universe it is a theorem. Concretely, the universe
carries a definable well-order, every nonempty set has a least member, and every
family of nonempty pairwise-disjoint sets admits an explicitly constructed
choice set. Finally, we establish the cumulative structure: the $\in$-transitive
closure of any set is finite, a genuine rank function exists and strictly
decreases along membership, and every set has rank at most its own code,
confirming containment in $V_\omega$. We outline the parallel programs of
negating Extensionality (theories of indistinguishable sets) and Foundation
(hyperset universes with Quine atoms).
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} Hereditarily finite sets, Ackermann coding, axiom of
choice, well-ordering, Separation, Replacement, $\in$-induction, cumulative rank,
anti-mathematics, negated axioms.

\section{Introduction}

Zermelo--Fraenkel set theory with Choice (ZFC) is the de facto foundation of
mathematics. Its axioms---Extensionality, Empty Set, Pairing, Union, Power Set,
Foundation, Infinity, the Separation and Replacement schemas, and Choice---are
usually treated as fixed bedrock. Yet each is a \emph{choice}, and a natural
counterfactual arises: for each axiom, what theory results from replacing it by
its negation?

We call this program \emph{anti-mathematics}. It is not the study of
inconsistency---the negated theories we consider are perfectly consistent---but
the study of the alternative mathematical universes that different axiomatic
commitments generate. Three negations organize the present work:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item $\neg\mathrm{Infinity}$ yields the hereditarily finite universe
    $\HF = V_\omega$ (the deep case, developed in full below);
  \item $\neg\mathrm{Extensionality}$ yields theories with distinct sets sharing
    identical members---indistinguishable duplicates;
  \item $\neg\mathrm{Foundation}$ yields hyperset universes containing
    self-membered sets (Quine atoms) and other non-well-founded objects.
\end{enumerate}

Our central technical vehicle is the Ackermann coding, which identifies the
hereditarily finite sets with the natural numbers via binary expansion. This
model turns abstract existence axioms into explicit finite computations and makes
every claim about $\HF$ verifiable by elementary arithmetic on $\mathbb{N}$.

The headline conceptual result is an \emph{interaction between negations}:
negating Infinity is not independent of Choice. In full ZF, Choice is
independent---both it and its negation are consistent. But in
$\mathrm{ZF} - \mathrm{Infinity} + \neg\mathrm{Infinity}$, Choice is a theorem.
Removing one axiom can convert a second, independent axiom into a provable fact.

\section{The Ackermann model}

\begin{definition}[Ackermann membership]
The Ackermann model has carrier $\mathbb{N}$. For $a, b \in \mathbb{N}$ define
\[
  a \meml b \quad :\Longleftrightarrow\quad \bit_a(b) = 1,
\]
where $\bit_a(b)$ is the $a$-th binary digit of $b$. Equivalently, writing
$b = \sum_i \bit_i(b)\,2^i$, we have $a \meml b$ iff $2^a$ appears in this sum.
\end{definition}

Thus each $b$ is read as the finite set of positions of the $1$s in its binary
representation: $0$ codes $\varnothing$; $1 = 2^0$ codes $\{\varnothing\}$;
$3 = 2^0 + 2^1$ codes $\{0,1\}$; $11 = 2^0+2^1+2^3$ codes $\{0,1,3\}$. The
correspondence between naturals and finite subsets of $\mathbb{N}$ is a
bijection.

\begin{lemma}[Membership decreases the code]\label{lem:memlt}
For all $a,b$, if $a \meml b$ then $a < b$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $\bit_a(b)=1$ then $b \ge 2^a$. Since $a < 2^a$ for every $a$, we get
$a < 2^a \le b$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:memlt} is the linchpin of the development: it makes membership
well-founded, so recursion terminates and transitive closures stay finite.

\begin{corollary}[No infinite descent, no self-membership]
There is no infinite chain $\cdots \meml a_1 \meml a_0$, and no $a$ with
$a \meml a$; both follow since $\meml$ embeds into $<$ on $\mathbb{N}$.
\end{corollary}

\paragraph{Finite axioms and failure of Infinity.} The finite ZF axioms hold by
direct construction on codes: Empty ($0$ has no set bits), Pairing
($2^a \mathbin{|} 2^b$ codes $\{a,b\}$), Union, Power Set, and Foundation. By
contrast, Infinity \emph{fails}: an inductive set would need members of unbounded
size, contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:memlt}. Hence the model realizes
$\mathrm{ZF} - \mathrm{Infinity} + \neg\mathrm{Infinity}$.

\section{The ZF schemas survive}

\begin{theorem}[$\in$-Induction schema]\label{thm:induction}
Let $P$ be a predicate on sets. If
$\forall a\,[(\forall x, x \meml a \to P(x)) \to P(a)]$, then $P(a)$ holds for
every set $a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By strong induction on $a \in \mathbb{N}$. Assume $P(x)$ for all $x < a$. For any
$x \meml a$, Lemma~\ref{lem:memlt} gives $x < a$, so $P(x)$; thus
$\forall x\,(x \meml a \to P(x))$, and the premise yields $P(a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fold bit test]\label{lem:fold}
Fix a decidable predicate $q$ and a function $g : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$. For
a finite list $L$ of naturals let
$M(L) = \bigor_{y \in L}\big(\text{if } q(y)\text{ then } 2^{g(y)}\text{ else } 0\big)$,
where $\bigor$ is iterated bitwise or. Then for every $z$,
\[
  \bit_z(M(L)) = 1 \iff \exists y \in L,\ q(y) \wedge g(y) = z.
\]
The case $g = \mathrm{id}$ gives $\bit_z(M(L)) = 1 \iff z \in L \wedge q(z)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $L$. The empty list gives $M = 0$; for $y :: L'$, the bit-$z$ value
is the or of $\bit_z(M(L'))$ with the head term's contribution, and a case split
on $q(y)$ and $g(y) = z$ closes the induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Separation schema]\label{thm:sep}
For every set $a$ and decidable predicate $p$, there is a set $s$ with
$\forall x,\ x \meml s \iff (x \meml a \wedge p(x))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $s = \bigor_{y < a}\big(\text{if } (\bit_y(a)=1 \wedge p(y))\text{ then } 2^y \text{ else } 0\big)$.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:fold} with $g=\mathrm{id}$, $\bit_x(s)=1$ iff $x<a$,
$\bit_x(a)=1$, and $p(x)$. Since $x \meml a$ already implies $x<a$, the range
restriction is free, giving $x \meml s \iff x \meml a \wedge p(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Replacement schema]\label{thm:rep}
For every set $a$ and function $F : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$, there is a set $s$
with $\forall y,\ y \meml s \iff \exists x\,(x \meml a \wedge y = F(x))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $s = \bigor_{x < a}\big(\text{if } \bit_x(a)=1 \text{ then } 2^{F(x)} \text{ else } 0\big)$.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:fold} with $q(x) = (\bit_x(a)=1)$ and $g = F$, $\bit_y(s)=1$
iff there is $x<a$ with $\bit_x(a)=1$ and $F(x)=y$; the range restriction is free
by Lemma~\ref{lem:memlt}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The Ackermann model satisfies every ZF axiom and schema except Infinity, and
satisfies $\neg\mathrm{Infinity}$. Hence
$\HF = \mathrm{ZF} - \mathrm{Infinity} + \neg\mathrm{Infinity}$ is a complete,
consistent set theory realized in $\mathbb{N}$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Negating Infinity makes Choice a theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Well-ordering of the universe]\label{thm:wo}
The Ackermann universe carries a definable strict well-order, namely $<$ on
codes; hence a global choice function exists.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(\mathbb{N},<)$ is a well-order, and the carrier of the model is $\mathbb{N}$
with $<$ definable.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nonempty sets have members]\label{lem:exmem}
If $b \ne 0$ then there exists $i$ with $i \meml b$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If no bit of $b$ were set, $b = 0$ by uniqueness of binary representation.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Least member]
For $b \ne 0$, let $\lm(b)$ be the least $i$ with $\bit_i(b)=1$ (and $\lm(0)=0$).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Least member]\label{thm:least}
Every nonempty set $a$ has an $\le$-least member: $\lm(a) \meml a$ and
$\forall x\,(x \meml a \to \lm(a) \le x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:exmem} the member set is nonempty; by well-ordering of
$\mathbb{N}$ it has a least element $\lm(a)$, which is a member and a lower bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Choice in the finite universe]\label{thm:choice}
Let $a$ be a set whose members are all nonempty and pairwise disjoint. Then there
is a choice set $c$ with
$\forall b\,(b \meml a \to \exists! x\,(x \meml c \wedge x \meml b))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Replacement (Theorem~\ref{thm:rep}) to $a$ with $F = \lm$; let $c$ be the
image, so $x \meml c$ iff $x = \lm(b')$ for some $b' \meml a$. Fix $b \meml a$.
Then $\lm(b) \meml c$ and $\lm(b) \meml b$ (as $b \ne 0$), giving existence. For
uniqueness, if $y \meml c$ and $y \meml b$, then $y = \lm(b')$ for some
$b' \meml a$; were $b' \ne b$, then $y \meml b'$ and $y \meml b$ would violate
disjointness, so $b' = b$ and $y = \lm(b)$.
\end{proof}

The choice set is \emph{computed} by selecting the least element of each member;
this is the sense in which negating Infinity resolves the status of Choice.

\section{Hereditary finiteness and the cumulative rank}

\begin{lemma}[Ancestors are smaller]\label{lem:anc}
If $x \mathrel{(\meml)^{+}} a$ (transitive closure), then $x < a$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on the derivation; the base is Lemma~\ref{lem:memlt}, and the step
chains two instances by transitivity of $<$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hereditary finiteness]\label{thm:hf}
For every $a$, the class of $\meml$-ancestors of $a$ is finite; equivalently the
transitive closure of $a$ is finite, so every set is hereditarily finite.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:anc} the ancestor class is contained in $\{x : x < a\}$, which
is finite.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every set has finitely many members.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Rank]
Define $\rank : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ by $\in$-recursion:
$\rank(a) = \sup\{\rank(x)+1 : x \meml a\}$, with $\sup\varnothing = 0$. The
recursion is well-founded by Lemma~\ref{lem:memlt}.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:rankmono}
$\rank(0) = 0$, and $x \meml a \implies \rank(x) < \rank(a)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The empty set has no members, so its supremum is $0$. If $x \meml a$, then
$\rank(x)+1$ is a term in the supremum defining $\rank(a)$, whence
$\rank(x) < \rank(x)+1 \le \rank(a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Containment in $V_\omega$]\label{thm:rankle}
Every set has rank at most its own code: $\rank(a) \le a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By strong induction on $a$. For each $x \meml a$ we have $x < a$ and, by
induction, $\rank(x) \le x < a$, so $\rank(x)+1 \le a$. Taking the supremum over
members preserves the bound, giving $\rank(a) \le a$.
\end{proof}

Since every rank is finite, the cumulative hierarchy never leaves the finite
stages: the model is contained in $V_\omega$, and combined with the schema
results it \emph{is} $V_\omega = \HF$.

\section{Two further anti-axioms}

\paragraph{Negating Extensionality.} Negating $\forall a,b\,[(\forall x, x\in a
\leftrightarrow x\in b) \to a=b]$ permits distinct sets with identical members---
e.g. two empty sets $\varnothing_1 \ne \varnothing_2$. The resulting theory
describes indistinguishable duplicates; forming the quotient by
indistinguishability recovers the standard hereditarily finite universe exactly
when membership is forced to be a congruence.

\paragraph{Negating Foundation.} Negating Foundation yields non-well-founded
(hyperset) universes. The simplest inhabitants are Quine atoms: sets $x$ with
$x = \{x\}$. A full development models such universes as coalgebras for the
finite-powerset functor and adopts an anti-foundation axiom (AFA): every directed
graph has a unique decoration by sets. AFA is consistent relative to ZF.

\section{Algorithms}

The Ackermann model makes set theory computational:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item \textbf{Decode/encode:} $b \mapsto \{a : \bit_a(b)=1\}$ and
    $S \mapsto \sum_{a\in S} 2^a$.
  \item \textbf{Separation:} $\bigor_{y<a,\ \bit_y(a)=1,\ p(y)} 2^y$.
  \item \textbf{Replacement:} $\bigor_{y<a,\ \bit_y(a)=1} 2^{F(y)}$.
  \item \textbf{Least member:} $\lm(b)$ is the number of trailing zeros of $b$.
  \item \textbf{Choice set:} $\bigor_{b\,\meml\,a} 2^{\lm(b)}$.
  \item \textbf{Rank:} memoized $\in$-recursion
    $\rank(a) = \max_{x\,\meml\,a}(\rank(x)+1)$, base $0$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}

Removing Infinity is not a subtraction but a substitution yielding a robust
world: every finite ZF axiom and both schemas survive, and the resulting universe
is exactly $V_\omega$, pinned down by a rank function bounded by the code. The
most interesting phenomenon is the interaction between negated and retained
axioms: negating Infinity converts Choice from an independent axiom into a
theorem---the logical status of an axiom is contingent on its neighbors. A
secondary theme is effectivity: Separation, Replacement, and Choice all reduce to
short explicit computations, so the finite universe is not merely consistent but
fully computable.

\section{Future work}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item Encode ordered pairs and functions as internal $\HF$ objects and re-prove
    Replacement and Choice with the selecting function delivered as an internal
    set.
  \item Formulate finite $\in$-recursion as an internal operator, prove the
    recursion theorem inside the model, and derive rank as an instance.
  \item Characterize the indistinguishability classes of the duplicated-empty-set
    universe and show precisely when the quotient recovers $\HF$.
  \item Build a Fraenkel--Mostowski permutation model with atoms in which a
    specified choice function provably fails---a finitary shadow of the
    ``all sets measurable'' phenomenon.
  \item Extend the Quine-atom universe to a full hyperset model as a coalgebra
    for the finite-powerset functor and prove AFA.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Anti-mathematics turns the fixed foundations of set theory into a space of
alternatives. Negating Infinity produces the hereditarily finite universe
$V_\omega$, realized in $\mathbb{N}$ via the Ackermann coding, in which every ZF
axiom but Infinity holds, the schemas reduce to bit manipulation, and---most
strikingly---the Axiom of Choice becomes a theorem, provable by taking least
elements. Negating Extensionality and Foundation open two further coherent
worlds. Together these results map a small but instructive region of the
landscape of possible mathematics.

\end{document}

